## 8. AGENT IMPLEMENTATION SEQUENCE

When feeding this document to an AI coding agent, instruct it to execute in 4 sequential steps:

1. **Step 1 (Data & Schemas):** Create `src/rpg-core/types/ruleset.types.ts` and `src/data/ruleset/default-ruleset.json` pre-populated with all 6 AD&D classes (Fighter, Ranger, Paladin, Cleric, Mage, Thief with full Levels 1–13 XP/THAC0/Spell Slot tables), 6 races (Human, Elf, Dwarf, Orc, Skeleton, Zombie), all 44 Eye of the Beholder II spells (Mage 1–6, Cleric 1–6), starter weapons/armor, sample monsters, and 2 premade parties.
2. **Step 2 (Rules Engine, Runtime & Batch Sim):** Implement `dice.ts`, `rulesEngine.ts`, `validator.ts`, `batchSimulator.ts`, and `RpgRuntime.ts` (including `patchActiveMember` and `fullRestoreParty`) with unit tests verifying THAC0, descending AC, Vancian spell consumption, Prime Requisite +10% XP, Multiclass XP splitting, and `2d6` Morale checks.
3. **Step 3 (Standalone Editor UI):** Build `<StatsEditorModal />` and all 8 panels using the blackened iron (`#17191B`) + aged brass (`#C6A365`) palette, `SharedNumberField`, `HU | EN` localization, and the `QuickTestPanel` (50-run batch sim + basic `d20`/`d6` roller).
4. **Step 4 (`voidcrew` Integration):** Add a `Stats Editor` button next to `Asset Manager` in `src/editor/EditorBar.tsx` and wire `RpgRuntime` into the game HUD/combat loop with `liveRuntime` passed into `<StatsEditorModal />`.